{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1315637744/9/codegen_prompts/7.md\"","aggregated_output":"你要为一个新的 ARC-AGI 风格题目实现代码。代码文件必须包含 `main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray` 和 `generate_input() -> np.ndarray`。\n\n## 新题位置\n\n- 描述目录：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1315637744/9`\n- 题号：`7`\n- 目标代码路径：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1315637744/9/7.py`\n\n## 新题描述\n\n<new_puzzle index=\"7\">\n- `description`：沿着路径上的彩色节点追踪行走方向，在每个节点处根据节点颜色在图例中查找转向指令，最终路径覆盖的区域形成输出。\n- `Input structure`：\n  - 背景为 black。\n  - 网格最左列是图例列：每个图例条目占两行，第一行是一个颜色像素（如 red），第二行同列位置放置一个 azure 箭头标记——箭头标记为一个像素，位于该图例颜色像素的正下方，其上/下/左/右偏移一格的位置有一个额外 azure 像素，两个 azure 像素合在一起指示转向方向（上=向左转，下=向右转）。图例条目之间空一行。\n  - 主网格区域（从第 2 列开始）包含一个 green 起点像素和一个 maroon 终点像素。\n  - 起点像素的正右方有一个 grey 像素，表示初始行走方向为向右。\n  - 主网格中散布若干彩色像素（颜色与图例中出现的颜色对应），作为路径节点。\n\n- `Rule`：\n  - 从 green 起点出发，沿初始方向（向右）逐格前进，将经过的背景像素涂为 grey。\n  - 当行进到一个彩色节点时，查图例获取该颜色对应的转向方向（左转或右转 90 度），改变行进方向，继续前进。\n  - 到达 maroon 终点或网格边界时停止。\n  - 保留所有彩色节点的原始颜色，路径涂为 grey。\n\n- `Key insight`：图例中两个 azure 像素的相对位置编码了转向方向，而主网格中的彩色节点是转向触发器——解题者需要先破译图例的方向编码方式，再在主网格中模拟路径行走，两层推理环环相扣。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n## 机制相似参考题\n\n下面参考题由本地相似度流程自动选择：先用 `annotations`/docstring/代码特征抽取机制标签，再按机制相似度、文本相似度和代码特征相似度重排，并用 MMR 做去重。优先学习这些题的对象识别方式、随机生成器约束、边界条件处理，不要照搬旧题规则。\n\n已选参考：eval/88e364bc, train/963c33f8, eval/dbff022c, train/5adee1b2, train/320afe60, eval/65b59efc, train/d968ffd4, train/17b866bd\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"88e364bc\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3253\nmechanism=0.3985\nlexical=0.0146\ncode_feature=0.4286\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:connected_components, object:closed_region, object:grid_edge, object:marker, object:path, object:template, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/88e364bc.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/88e364bc.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nSlide yellow pixels inside walled regions in the direction dictated by border-legend arrows.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color; yellow pixels sit inside enclosed regions bounded by colored walls.\n- Border legends: small rectangles on the grid edge whose fill color C matches a region's border color. Each legend contains a center dot and an indicator dot encoding a direction.\n\nRule:\n- For each enclosed region, identify its border color C and look up the legend with fill color C.\n- The direction from the legend's center dot to its indicator dot gives the slide direction.\n- All yellow pixels in that region slide in that direction, stacking against the far wall.\n\nKey insight: Legend fill color links to region border color; the center→indicator offset encodes the movement vector.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"summary\": \"Yellow pixels inside colored-border regions move in the direction indicated by the red pixel in the legend with matching background color, stopping at region boundary.\",\n  \"type\": \"move_along_path\",\n  \"src\": \"yellow pixels inside colored-border regions s\",\n  \"dst\": null,\n  \"match_cond\": \"\",\n  \"path\": \"move(s, direction=legend.red_pixel_offset) where legend.background_color == s.containing_region.border_color until s.touches(region_boundary)\",\n  \"trajectory\": \"\",\n  \"transform\": \"\",\n  \"symbol_instructions\": \"legend on grid edge with background color C and red pixel indicates: yellow pixels in regions with border color C move in direction from blue center to red pixel\",\n  \"multi_step\": \"\",\n  \"concepts\": [\n    \"legend/control-pixels\",\n    \"region-membership\",\n    \"directional-movement\",\n    \"boundary-collision\"\n  ],\n  \"notes\": \"\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"963c33f8\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.2989\nmechanism=0.3642\nlexical=0.0164\ncode_feature=0.4000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, object:grid_edge, object:marker, object:path, relation:between, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/963c33f8.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/963c33f8.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nDrop the columns of the top marker downward, stopping blue-ended columns on grey obstacles and letting maroon-ended columns pass to the bottom.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- The most numerous non-background color forms stationary grey obstacle pixels.\n- The other two colors form a single 3x3 top-edge marker whose top two rows\n  are maroon and whose bottom row contains maroon or blue control pixels.\n\nRule\n- Erase the top marker.\n- Treat each marker column as a vertical drop retaining its three colors.\n- A column whose bottom control pixel is blue stops immediately above the\n  first grey pixel below that column, or bottom-aligns if none exists.\n- Contiguous maroon-ended columns pass through obstacles and bottom-align;\n  if such a run has a clear down-right slide from a grey ledge, shift the\n  run one column right before bottom-aligning.\n- When a maroon-ended drop lands through a grey bar on the bottom edge, clear\n  the rest of the marker-width gap in that bottom bar.\n\nKey insight\n- The bottom row of the top marker is a control strip: blue cells mean\n  \"stick to the first obstacle,\" while maroon cells mean \"fall through.\"\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_along_path\",\n  \"src\": \"columns of the 3x3 maroon/blue marker on the top edge; the bottom cell of each column is the control pixel\",\n  \"path\": \"vertical drop in each marker column; blue-ended columns stop above the first grey obstacle below, while maroon-ended runs pass through to the bottom with a one-column right slide from a clear grey ledge\",\n  \"trajectory\": \"copy each three-pixel marker column intact; contiguous maroon-ended columns are tested as one run for the slide\",\n  \"dst\": \"settled positions on the same canvas after erasing the top marker\",\n  \"transform\": \"if a maroon-ended drop lands through a grey bar on the bottom edge, clear the rest of the marker-width gap in that bar; leave other grey obstacles fixed\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"dbff022c\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3090\nmechanism=0.3451\nlexical=0.0119\ncode_feature=0.6000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:connected_components, object:closed_region, object:grid_edge, object:path, object:template, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/dbff022c.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/dbff022c.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nFill hollow sprites' interiors using a color-palette legend on the grid edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Several hollow sprites with distinct border colors.\n- A small 2×N or N×2 palette block sits on one grid edge; one row/column lists border colors, the other lists corresponding fill colors.\n\nRule:\n- Locate the palette legend on a grid edge and read it as a border→fill color mapping.\n- Find enclosed bg regions (holes) inside sprites that don't touch the grid boundary.\n- For each hole, identify the most common adjacent border color, look it up in the palette, and fill the hole with the mapped fill color.\n\nKey insight: The palette's edge position and orientation determine reading direction; border colors in the palette uniquely key into fill colors.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"summary\": \"Use a color palette legend on the grid edge to fill sprites' interiors based on their border color mapping.\",\n  \"type\": \"transform_in_place\",\n  \"src\": \"hollow sprites with colored borders s\",\n  \"dst\": \"interior of same sprite d\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"d.fill_color == palette_lookup(s.border_color)\",\n  \"path\": \"\",\n  \"trajectory\": \"\",\n  \"transform\": \"fill d with palette[s.border_color]\",\n  \"symbol_instructions\": \"palette is 2xN or Nx2 block on grid edge; read direction based on edge position: top edge reads top-to-bottom, bottom edge reads bottom-to-top, left edge reads left-to-right; first row/column is border color, second is fill color\",\n  \"multi_step\": \"Step 1: locate 2xN or Nx2 palette legend on grid edge; Step 2: build color mapping (border -> fill) based on reading direction; Step 3: for each hollow sprite, lookup border color in palette and fill interior with corresponding fill color\",\n  \"concepts\": [\n    \"color-mapping\",\n    \"lookup-table\",\n    \"flood-fill\",\n    \"border-detection\"\n  ],\n  \"notes\": \"The challenge is correctly reading the palette legend based on its edge position and orientation\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"5adee1b2\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.2630\nmechanism=0.3125\nlexical=0.0125\ncode_feature=0.4000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, object:closed_region, object:grid_edge, object:marker, object:template, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/5adee1b2.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/5adee1b2.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nUse the left-edge color legend to draw a padded rectangular halo around each matching object while preserving enclosed holes.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has a dominant background color.\n- One or more two-row markers sit on the left edge; each marker is a 2x2 block whose left column is an object color and right column is its halo color.\n- Away from the markers, every source color from the legend appears as one or more 4-connected monochrome objects.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the input pixels unchanged.\n- For each source-color object, take its tight bbox expanded by one cell and clipped to the grid.\n- Recolor background pixels in that expanded region to the marker's halo color, except for background holes enclosed by the object.\n\nKey insight:\n- The small left-edge bars are a legend: they map object colors to the colors used for the surrounding padded bbox.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"transform_in_place\",\n  \"src\": \"4-connected monochrome objects on the background plus left-edge two-column color legend markers\",\n  \"transform\": \"for each object whose color appears in the legend, fill background pixels in its one-cell padded bbox with the paired legend color; preserve the object and any enclosed background holes\",\n  \"symbol_instructions\": \"left-edge two-column legend maps object color to bbox-fill color\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"320afe60\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.2544\nmechanism=0.2984\nlexical=0.0174\ncode_feature=0.4000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, object:grid_edge, object:path, relation:aligned, relation:between, relation:direction, relation:overlap, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/320afe60.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/320afe60.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nSlide each foreground object horizontally to the edge indicated by whether its shape has an open notch.\n\nInput structure:\n- Yellow is the background and there is one blue foreground color.\n- The foreground is split into separated 4-connected objects.\n- Each object keeps its row span and shape in the output.\n\nRule:\n- If an object's bbox border contains a background gap flanked by object pixels along that same border, slide it to the right edge and recolor it green.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
